# Readable code, shallow nesting

Clean code is easier to maintain, read, and hand to the next developer. Prefer a straight path over nested branches.

## Flatten a pattern that already repeats

When the same multi-line indented check shows up in more than one place, replace it with one named call. `ArgumentHelpers.ThrowIfNull` (and the other `ThrowIf*` helpers) is the model: a null check plus a throw becomes a single line.

```csharp
// ❌ same block copied into several methods
if (services is null)
    throw new ArgumentNullException(nameof(services));

// ✅ one call, same meaning
ArgumentHelpers.ThrowIfNull(services);
```

Use an existing helper before adding a new one. Name a new helper for the condition it enforces.

## Leave a one-off inline

A pattern that appears once stays inline. A private method or a new helper is worth it only when a second call site already exists, or is part of the same change. Do not add an abstraction in case it is reused later.

## Keep the happy path shallow

Guard and return or throw at the top so the rest of the method is the main path. Do not wrap the body in `if` or `try` when a guard does the same job.
